  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nLooking at current water vapor satellite imagery and the analyzed \nsynoptic pattern over the CONUS this afternoon, a sharp shortwave \ntrough is seen over the West Coast with a drying and subsident \npattern caused by upper-level ridge extending from the Southern High \nPlains into the Ohio River Valley. The ridge is strongly positively \ntilted, helping to trap an upper-level low pressure system just off \nthe central Gulf Coast as we move into the day tomorrow. Continued \neasterly flow between these two features is expanding low-level \nhumidity into our region, but actual conditions will remain dry. \nWeak returns on radar indicate  mostly evaporating precipitation \nover southern Alabama this afternoon, for example, but a few trace \nto 0.01\" per hour obs have been reported. Can't rule out a sprinkle \nin a part of our area, but we will stay on the mostly drier western \nside of the low pressure system as it lifts northward Thursday.\n\nThe pattern Friday into the weekend should at least see the \nprevailing influence of high pressure pull away from our region. A \nstrengthening southern jet stream will expand eastward from the \nPacific along the Gulf Coast, perhaps providing some ascent in the \nregion or at least helping to usher a shortwave trough across the \nInterstate 20/40 corridor. Confidence in measurable rainfall is low, \nbut can't rule out sprinkles or a little light rain at some point \nduring the weekend.\n\nRounding out the forecast next Monday and Tuesday, not much can be \nsaid with certainty. The pattern is expected to remain progressive \ninto the middle of next week. Likely impact is for at least a \nreinforcing cold front early in the week. After trending warm with \nhighs in the 70s and lows in the 50s late this week and weekend (10 \ndegrees above normal), a reinforcing cold front would nudge us back \ntoward normal with mostly 60s/40s temperatures for the early week \ntime frame. /NF/",
